menu "Wearable"

    config GADGET_HUB_MAC
        string "MAC of the room hub"
        default ""
        help
            "aa:bb:cc:dd:ee:ff", the station MAC the hub prints at boot. Empty: no
            hub (a bench or a demo with this board only). ESP-NOW is then not
            started, and falls go to the serial log (and to MQTT and CAN when they
            are on). See sdkconfig.demo.defaults. A value of the wrong length fails
            the build.

    config GADGET_ESPNOW_ENCRYPT
        bool "Encrypt ESP-NOW (PMK + LMK)"
        default y
        help
            Must match the hub: same PMK, and this wearable's MAC configured there.

    config GADGET_ESPNOW_PMK
        string "Primary master key (16 characters)"
        default "pmk-change-me-16"
        help
            Exactly 16 characters, the same on the hub; another length fails the build.

    config GADGET_ESPNOW_LMK
        string "Local master key (16 characters)"
        default "lmk-change-me-16"
        help
            Exactly 16 characters, the same on the hub; another length fails the build.

    config GADGET_FALL_TRIGGER_CG
        int "Fall trigger: peak of filtered |a| (L_on), in hundredths of g"
        range 120 800
        default 200
        help
            A peak of |a| (20 Hz low-pass) at or above this wakes the classifier
            (200 = 2.0 g). The trigger decides nothing: running or sitting down hard
            cross it too, and the classifier tells them from a fall. 2.0 g is what
            the models were trained with (SisFall at +-8 g: 99.2 % of the falls cross
            it); changing it moves the windows away from the training data. Revisit
            it with the wrist data (modelo/experimentos/02_gatilho.py), then retrain.

    choice GADGET_FALL_CLASSIFIER
        prompt "Fall classifier"
        default GADGET_FALL_CLASSIFIER_RF
        help
            Both come from modelo/experimentos/09_exportar_vestivel.py (SisFall,
            waist, +-8 g) and run on the same 23 features. They are provisional
            until retrained with the wrist data (docs/pendencias.md).

        config GADGET_FALL_CLASSIFIER_RF
            bool "Random forest (60 trees): the chosen model"

        config GADGET_FALL_CLASSIFIER_LR
            bool "Logistic regression (23 weights)"
    endchoice

    config GADGET_FALL_COOLDOWN_S
        int "Seconds after a reported fall before another one is reported"
        range 1 600
        default 30

    config GADGET_CANCEL_WINDOW_S
        int "Seconds the resident has to cancel a fall with the button"
        range 0 120
        default 15

    config GADGET_BUTTON_SIMULATES_FALL
        bool "Button simulates a fall when none is pending (tests only)"
        default n
        help
            Exercises the whole path wearable -> hub -> central -> Telegram without
            having to fall. Leave off in use.

    config GADGET_COLLECT_MODE
        bool "Data collection mode: print every raw sample as CSV"
        default n
        help
            Prints "CSV:t_ms,ax,ay,az,gx,gy,gz" in raw counts at 200 Hz, for building
            the dataset of the fall model (docs/coleta-de-dados.md), and a "FEAT:"
            line per trigger with its 23 features, P(fall) and the CPU cycles.

    config GADGET_BATTERY_SENSE
        bool "Battery voltage divider fitted"
        default n
        help
            The single prototype PCB has no divider: the wearable runs from a power bank
            on the board's USB-C input, and heartbeats report the battery as unknown
            (0xFFFF), so the hub skips the low-battery rule. Turn it on for a board with
            a LiPo and a divider on PIN_BATTERY.

    config GADGET_BATTERY_DIVIDER_X1000
        int "Divider ratio (battery / pin voltage) x 1000"
        depends on GADGET_BATTERY_SENSE
        range 1000 10000
        default 2000

    config GADGET_MQTT
        bool "Also join the Wi-Fi and talk to the central over MQTT"
        default n
        help
            The wearable joins APP_WIFI_SSID (menu "Network (Wi-Fi)") and the broker
            (menu "MQTT", user "vestivel"). ESP-NOW to the hub stays the main path and
            then rides on the access point's channel. Over MQTT the wearable publishes
            its own telemetry and, when the hub does not acknowledge a fall even after
            looking for it, publishes the fall alert itself (docs/protocolo.md §1).
            Costs battery: the radio stays associated.

    config GADGET_CAN
        bool "CAN bus (bench tests and course requirement only)"
        default n
        help
            Sends falls and heartbeats as CAN frames 0x0FD / 0x0FE (docs/protocolo.md
            §2) through a transceiver on PIN_CAN_TX / PIN_CAN_RX, and logs what the
            other nodes send. Not part of the product: the wearable has no transceiver.

endmenu
